<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The problem was that whe=
n the WebSocket library called <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0=
.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] p=
x-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">handlePriceUpdate</code>, the <code class=3D"bg-te=
xt-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre=
-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">this</code> context was lo=
st</strong> - it was being called as a plain function, not as a method of o=
ur <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-da=
nger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Tra=
dingFeed</code> instance. So <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5=
 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-=
1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">this</code> was either <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 =
border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rou=
nded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">undefined</code> (in strict mode) o=
r pointed to the global object.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent example! You've identified the core issue - the context loss when p=
assing methods as callbacks. This is incredibly common in frontend developm=
ent, especially with event handlers and async operations.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I can see you understand the fun=
damental principle that <strong><code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-=
0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] =
px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">this</code> is determined by the call-site, not t=
he definition site</strong>. Now, let me push you a bit further. You mentio=
ned this was in production - what were the actual symptoms your users exper=
ienced, and more importantly, how did you diagnose this? Because <code clas=
s=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whit=
espace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">this</code> issu=
es can be quite subtle and might not always throw obvious errors.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question! The symptoms were actually quite insidious, which made it chall=
enging to diagnose initially.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>User-facing symptoms:</s=
trong></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Price updates would appear to w=
ork for the first few seconds after a user subscribed to an instrument</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Then updates would mysteriously=
 stop, but only for certain instruments and only for some users</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">No obvious error messages in th=
e console initially</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Users would refresh the page an=
d everything would work again temporarily</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The diagnostic challenge=
</strong> was that the error only occurred when the WebSocket received actu=
al price updates from our backend. During development, we were mostly testi=
ng with mock data, so we didn't trigger the real WebSocket callback mechani=
sm.</p>
