<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7" start=3D"3">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance Considerati=
ons for Our Use Case</strong></li>
</ol>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">We typically have <strong>one i=
nstance</strong> of <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">TradingFeed</code> per trading session</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">But we register <strong>many ev=
ent listeners</strong> during the session lifecycle</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Class fields meant <strong>one =
function per instance</strong>, but <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bor=
der-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4r=
em] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">.bind()</code> would create <strong>one funct=
ion per event registration</strong></li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Trade-off Analysis:</str=
ong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Memory</strong>:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Class fields use slightly more =
memory per instance (functions aren't shared via prototype)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">But for our singleton-like usag=
e pattern, this was negligible</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Actually <strong>saved memory</=
strong> compared to multiple <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5=
 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-=
1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">.bind()</code> calls</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance</strong>:</p=
>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Fastest execution</stro=
ng> - direct function call, no binding overhead</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Critical for high-frequency pri=
ce updates (10k+/sec)</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Bundle Size</strong>:</p=
>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Babel transforms class fields i=
nto constructor assignments</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Slightly larger transpiled code=
, but the difference was minimal with our build optimization</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ou=
tstanding analysis! I'm particularly impressed by your consideration of the=
 TypeScript implications and the performance characteristics for high-frequ=
ency updates. The point about <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.=
5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px=
-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">.bind()</code> creating multiple function objects v=
s. class fields creating one per instance is a subtle but important distinc=
tion that many developers miss.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">This brings up an interesting fo=
llow-up: <strong>In your real-time trading system, you mentioned 10,000+ up=
dates per second. At that scale, even small performance differences can com=
pound. Have you done any actual performance benchmarking of these different=
 approaches?</strong> And more broadly, how do you approach performance opt=
imization in React components when dealing with such high-frequency updates=
?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Also, I'm curious about <strong>=
edge cases</strong>. Can you think of scenarios where the class field appro=
ach might actually be problematic or where you'd choose one of the other ap=
proaches instead?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Exce=
llent questions! Yes, we absolutely benchmarked this - at 10k+ updates/seco=
nd, micro-optimizations really matter.</p>
